From ac213e7b583699585d2be1514bd38b1c3f9f3ff4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Pooya Parsa <pooya@pi0.io>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 10:16:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] wasm: sysrq: observe keyboards passively instead of opening
 them

With MAGIC_SYSRQ, sysrq's input handler connects to every device with
KEY_LEFTALT and opens it. The open of the kwinput keyboard is what tells
the host that a program reads it (wasm_driver_input_state()), and the
host then sends its key presses to the keyboard instead of the console,
so from boot the keyboard looked open with nobody reading it, and the
page would have sent every keystroke to evdev.

Make the handler a passive observer on wasm: it still sees the events of
a keyboard that something else (evdev) has open, so Alt-SysRq works
while a program reads the keyboard, but it no longer starts the device.
There is no VT keyboard here, and the host queues no keys for a closed
device, so sysrq loses nothing. /proc/sysrq-trigger is unchanged.

A default enable mask of 0 would not do: the handler is registered again
as soon as kernel.sysrq is set non-zero, which systemd's 50-default.conf
(kernel.sysrq = 16) and Ubuntu's 10-magic-sysrq.conf do.
---
 drivers/tty/sysrq.c | 7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/tty/sysrq.c b/drivers/tty/sysrq.c
index c2e4b31b6..954cc3966 100644
--- a/drivers/tty/sysrq.c
+++ b/drivers/tty/sysrq.c
@@ -1031,10 +1031,17 @@ static const struct input_device_id sysrq_ids[] = {
 	{ },
 };
 
+/*
+ * On wasm the keyboards are the host's (kwinput, no VT): opening one tells
+ * the host that a program reads it, and the host then sends its key presses
+ * there instead of to the console. So only watch keyboards that something
+ * else opened; Alt-SysRq still works while a program reads the keyboard.
+ */
 static struct input_handler sysrq_handler = {
 	.filter		= sysrq_filter,
 	.connect	= sysrq_connect,
 	.disconnect	= sysrq_disconnect,
+	.passive_observer = IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_WASM),
 	.name		= "sysrq",
 	.id_table	= sysrq_ids,
 };
